<p>Nasa’s Artemis II crew is now committed to its lunar fly-by after a major engine firing sent the Orion spacecraft out of Earth orbit and onto its path around the Moon. The burn marks humanity’s first crewed journey beyond Earth orbit since Apollo 17 in 1972. The 10-day mission launched from Kennedy Space Center in Florida on April 1 with four astronauts aboard: Reid Wiseman, Victor Glover, Christina Koch and Canadian Space Agency astronaut Jeremy Hansen. This mission is a test flight, not a landing attempt, with Nasa using it to validate the systems and procedures it will need for lunar surface missions later in the decade. Early in the flight, the crew stayed in Earth orbit to check the spacecraft and work through minor issues, including a toilet malfunction, before the successful burn put them on course for the Moon.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/8HmqPVjm9jYa1aK-06YGkWoB8g9Egj91FcW_-vEpYKzxWnvrdLWQ8rBGZstMa1Dr82Cw4ectExWFDJhis9ZjdHvzc3DEXNSdtFJ5UtiBPMUlSrg2RHoPtnnwTzMrnXC-P0k1UKAcGMTKXd6SfZ-qEi6DfxHssznhstqgEh3SkWnwInPoOkdZQHbmOMsqBg-3X8vQoPHrsHi4UR7G_ucyK-7sHPl9KrnycOOnytOt4YLWPHiGaFBPTL9PNg' alt='In this photo provided by NASA, a view of the Earth from NASA&#x27;s Orion spacecraft as it orbits above the planet during the Artemis II test flight, on Thursday, April 2, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Crew roles: Reid Wiseman is commander, Victor Glover is pilot, and Christina Koch and Jeremy Hansen are mission specialists, showing how responsibilities are divided aboard Orion.</li><li>Deep-space vehicle: Cited coverage describes Orion as the largest spacecraft built to carry humans into deep space.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Nasa: The agency is treating Artemis II as a step-by-step rehearsal, using a crewed lunar fly-by to prove Orion and deep-space procedures before taking on the added complexity of a landing attempt later in the decade. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/videos/c3w344wyxg5o'>BBC News</a>)</li><li>The New York Times analysis: The mission is framed not just as exploration, but as part of a high-stakes competition between the United States and China over the future of the Moon. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/02/world/space-artemis-us-china-trump-iran-pam-bondi.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>WSWS analysis: The outlet argues that geopolitical rivalry with China, more than science alone, is driving the Artemis program’s urgency. (<a href='https://www.wsws.org/en/articles/2026/04/03/uidr-a03.html'>World Socialist Web Site</a>)</li><li>BBC reporting: Coverage also focuses on day-to-day operational questions such as sleep windows, onboard toilet reliability and routine spacecraft checks — practical signs of whether Orion can support a crew smoothly in deep space. (<a href='https://www.allsides.com/news/2026-04-02-0700/science-artemis-ii-orbit-what-happens-next'>AllSides citing BBC</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/united-states-canada/article/3348862/nasas-artemis-crew-gets-green-light-make-big-push-moon'>Nasa’s Artemis astronauts zoom towards moon after fixing toilet malfunction</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c937g7nd5x4o'>Artemis II blasts ever closer to the far side of the Moon</a> - bbc.com</li><li><a href='https://www.allsides.com/news/2026-04-02-0700/science-artemis-ii-orbit-what-happens-next'>Artemis II is in orbit - what happens next?</a> - allsides.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/us-politics/article-analysis-artemis-iran-trump/'>Artemis II and Iran: Two strikingly different missions define the U.S.</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://www.rt.com/news/637090-nasa-artemis-moon-mission/'>Artemis II: Why is the US returning to the Moon?</a> - rt.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/artemis-ii-astronauts-leave-earth-s-orbit-rocket-towards-moon/a-76649996'>Artemis II astronauts leave Earth&#x27;s orbit, rocket towards moon</a> - dw.com